---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { SECTIONS } from "../data/sections";

const title = "RHYTHM FUN Treadmill (2026): Incline Walking Pads Checked";
const description =
  "Is a RHYTHM FUN treadmill or incline walking pad worth buying? RHYTHM FUN’s range and prices checked on 5 November 2026, the discontinued AP01 and AP02, conflicting figures and alternatives.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    id: "rhythm-fun-16-incline-walking-pad-handles",
    name: "RHYTHM FUN 6.2MPH Walking Pad with Handles, 16% Incline",
    brand: "RHYTHM FUN",
    category: "RHYTHM FUN’s Side-Handlebar Incline Pad on Amazon",
    badge: "Closest on Amazon",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0GMQXG1L3?tag=sktiger-20&linkCode=ll1",
    amazonLabel: "Check Price on Amazon",
    image: {"url":"https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/41W9q1HnBxL._SL500_.jpg","width":478,"height":500},
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "$169.99 (Amazon, 9 Nov 2026); the 13% pad was $209.99 at RHYTHM FUN on 5 Nov 2026",
    priceBounds: {"low":169.99,"high":209.99},
    specs: "0.6–6.2 mph · 300 lb capacity · 37.5 x 15 in. belt · folds to 3.9 in. · 8.5 in. side handlebars · incline 16% (Amazon title) or 13% (RHYTHM FUN store)",
    pros: [
      "Its Amazon title gives the same 6.2 mph, 300 lb, 37.5-inch belt, 3.9-inch fold and 8.5-inch side handlebars as RHYTHM FUN’s 13% pad",
      "Two short side handlebars rather than a single upright",
      "A manual incline, which few pads at this price offer",
      "About $40 less on Amazon than the 13% pad on RHYTHM FUN’s store",
    ],
    cons: [
      "The Amazon title says 16% incline and RHYTHM FUN’s store says 13% for the matching pad; we print neither as confirmed",
      "A 15-inch belt is narrow for a brisk walk",
      "A one-year warranty and no published motor rating",
    ],
    bottomLine: "The RHYTHM FUN incline pad with side handlebars that Amazon sells; check the incline positions in the listing photos before you order.",
    description: "RHYTHM FUN’s store sells the 13% incline walking pad that this guide recommends, but we could not find an Amazon US listing under that name. The closest Amazon listing, from RHYTHM FUN itself, is titled as a 6.2 mph walking pad with handles and a 16% incline, and its title gives every other headline figure of the 13% pad: 0.6 to 6.2 mph, a 37.5-inch belt, a 3.9-inch folded height, 8.5-inch foldable side handlebars, app connection and a 300 lb capacity. RHYTHM FUN’s US store, on 5 November 2026, gave the 13% pad three manual incline positions (0, 10 and 13%), the same speed, capacity, belt and fold, front transport wheels and the YPOOFIT app, and an independent listing summary of its Amazon listing agreed on 0.6 to 6.2 mph, 300 lb, a 37.5 by 15-inch belt and a 3.9-inch fold, plus a 40.8 lb unit weight, but gave no incline figure. So the two are probably the same pad or a close revision, but the incline maximum conflicts, 16% against 13%, and we could not settle it. RHYTHM FUN publishes no motor rating. Amazon listed it at $169.99 on 9 November 2026.",
  },
  {
    id: "deerrun-a6-plus",
    name: "DeerRun A6 Plus",
    brand: "DeerRun",
    category: "Closest on Amazon to RHYTHM FUN’s 3.0 HP Treadmill: a 2-in-1 for Light Jogging",
    badge: "Closest on Amazon",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0HKMRVKCM?tag=sktiger-20&linkCode=ll1",
    amazonLabel: "Check Price on Amazon",
    image: {"url":"https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/51lOM7bW9jL._SL500_.jpg","width":500,"height":500},
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "About $249 (Amazon, 6 Nov 2026)",
    priceBounds: {"low":249,"high":249},
    specs: "0.6–7.5 mph · 5% manual incline · 300 lb capacity (listing) · fold-flat with handrail · PitPat app and remote",
    pros: [
      "A 7.5 mph top speed, the same as RHYTHM FUN’s 3.0 HP treadmill, with a handrail",
      "A 300 lb maximum weight stated plainly in the listing",
      "A 5% manual incline for about $249",
      "Works with the PitPat app for tracking and virtual runs",
    ],
    cons: [
      "The listing says \"longer running belt\" but gives no length",
      "It does not separate walking-mode and running-mode speeds in the bullets",
      "DeerRun sells many similar models with similar titles, so check the A6 Plus name in your basket",
    ],
    bottomLine: "For light jogging with a handrail, at a price close to the RHYTHM FUN’s lower listing. Confirm the belt length with DeerRun if you plan to jog.",
    description: "RHYTHM FUN’s 3.0 HP treadmill with handles is listed twice on its own store, and we could not find an Amazon US listing that matches it, so this card shows the closest light-jogging 2-in-1 that Amazon does sell. The DeerRun A6 Plus is a walking pad and running machine with a handrail. Its Amazon listing describes 0.6 to 7.5 mph, a manual incline that the title gives as 5%, a 300 lb maximum weight, an alloy steel frame, a shock-absorbing deck, a fold-flat design, a full-screen display, a remote and the PitPat app. Two gaps are worth knowing. It calls the belt longer without saying how long, and it gives a single speed range rather than separate walking and running modes. Like the RHYTHM FUN, it is a jogging allowance on a short deck, not a running treadmill, and its incline is shallower than the RHYTHM FUN’s claimed 13%. DeerRun sells many similar models, so check the A6 Plus name in your basket. Amazon showed it at $249.00 on 6 November 2026.",
  },
  {
    id: "ancheer-walking-pad-handle-bar",
    name: "ANCHEER Walking Pad with Handle Bar and 12% Incline",
    brand: "ANCHEER",
    category: "Closest on Amazon to RHYTHM FUN’s Cheapest Pad: a Handle-Bar Pad With Incline",
    badge: "Closest on Amazon",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0HBW4J9N9?tag=sktiger-20&linkCode=ll1",
    amazonLabel: "Check Price on Amazon",
    image: {"url":"https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/41CghfnkBcL._SL500_.jpg","width":500,"height":500},
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "Amazon price not confirmed on 5 Nov 2026",
    priceBounds: null,
    specs: "0.6–4.0 mph · 2-level manual incline to 12% · 16 in. belt width · fold-down handlebar",
    pros: [
      "A handlebar for balance, which most flat pads lack",
      "Manual incline to 12% for steeper walking",
      "Price-tracked on Amazon between about $151 and $180 for most of 2026",
      "Sold by Amazon.com on the tracked listing, so Amazon’s return terms apply",
    ],
    cons: [
      "The title says 320 lb and the feature list 350 lb; we print neither",
      "The title says 3.0 HP; the feature list says 1000 W, about 1.3 HP",
      "No independent review or brand website to check against",
    ],
    bottomLine: "For a light walker who wants a handle bar and a manual incline for under $200, planning on the lower capacity and motor figures.",
    description: "RHYTHM FUN’s cheapest handle-bar pad, $199.99 on its own store, could not be matched to an Amazon US listing, so this card shows the closest cheap handle-bar pad with incline that Amazon does sell. ANCHEER’s pad, tracked by PriceHistory on Amazon and sold by Amazon.com, has a feature list giving a 1000 W brushless motor, 0.6 to 4.0 mph, a 16-inch belt, a two-level manual incline to 12%, an 18 kg (about 40 lb) weight and a 4.2-inch folded height, about the same weight as the RHYTHM FUN and slimmer folded. The same listing contradicts itself twice: its title says 3.0 HP and 320 lb, its feature list 1000 W, about 1.3 HP, and 350 lb. Plan on 320 lb or less. Like the RHYTHM FUN, it rests on the seller’s figures alone, with no independent review. PriceHistory recorded a low of $151.99 in May 2026 and $167.99 after a price change on 21 June 2026, against a stated $329.99. Buy it from the Amazon-sold listing so that a fault in the first month is Amazon’s problem.",
  },
  {
    id: "rhythm-fun-s8",
    name: "RHYTHM FUN S8 Treadmill with 8% Incline",
    brand: "RHYTHM FUN",
    category: "Folding Treadmill With Speaker",
    badge: "Most Features",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0F6SYHVR2?tag=sktiger-20&linkCode=ll1",
    amazonLabel: "Check Price on Amazon",
    image: {"url":"https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/411yqxsOuRL._SL500_.jpg","width":500,"height":500},
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "$490.99 at RHYTHM FUN (5 Nov 2026)",
    priceBounds: {"low":490.99,"high":490.99},
    specs: "Maker’s figures: 8 mph · 300 lb · three-position manual incline to 8% · brushless motor · Bluetooth speaker",
    pros: [
      "A Bluetooth speaker, phone holder and remote",
      "The highest top speed RHYTHM FUN lists, 8 mph",
      "A brushless motor, as RHYTHM FUN describes it",
    ],
    cons: [
      "At $490.99 it competes with better-documented folding treadmills",
      "RHYTHM FUN gives noise “below 65 dB”, louder than its walking pads",
      "No belt size, motor rating or weight published",
    ],
    bottomLine: "Hard to recommend at this price over a folding treadmill whose deck size and motor are published.",
    description: "The S8 is the most expensive treadmill on RHYTHM FUN’s store, listed at $490.99, against a $699.99 “regular” price, on 5 November 2026. RHYTHM FUN describes a handle-bar treadmill with a three-lever manual incline to 8%, speeds to 8 mph, a 300 lb capacity, a brushless motor with noise below 65 dB, a Bluetooth speaker, a phone holder, a remote and the YPOOFIT app, but gives no belt size, motor rating or unit weight. Its description also calls it a “YPOO treadmill”, which suggests the same machine is sold under the YPOO name. At this price, a folding treadmill with a published deck size and continuous motor rating is a safer buy; our best folding treadmills roundup lists several.",
  },
  {
    id: "merach-novawalk-w50-trekpad",
    name: "MERACH NovaWalk W50 TrekPad",
    brand: "MERACH",
    category: "Checked Incline Pad Alternative",
    badge: "Best Checked Alternative",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0HJTT2S6R?tag=sktiger-20&linkCode=ll1",
    amazonLabel: "Check Price on Amazon",
    image: {"url":"https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/41N86eQUrbL._SL500_.jpg","width":500,"height":500},
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "$299.99 at MERACH (5 Nov 2026)",
    priceBounds: {"low":299.99,"high":299.99},
    specs: "41.3 x 16.5 in. belt · 0.6–4.0 mph · 400 lb capacity · 1.25 HP continuous motor · powered incline",
    pros: [
      "A 400 lb rating that holds up across the maker, Amazon and independent coverage",
      "A powered incline, adjusted without stepping off",
      "A two-year warranty, double RHYTHM FUN’s",
    ],
    cons: [
      "No handlebars",
      "Does not fold, and at 69 lb is heavy to move",
      "MERACH gives 12% incline and at least one roundup 10%, so no maximum is printed",
    ],
    bottomLine: "The incline pad to buy if you want a longer, wider belt and figures someone else has checked.",
    description: "If an incline walking pad is what you want and you would rather not rely on a maker’s figures alone, this is the checked comparison. MERACH’s product page on 5 November 2026 listed the W50 TrekPad at $299.99, against $479.99, with a 41.3 by 16.5-inch belt, longer and wider than any RHYTHM FUN, 0.6 to 4.0 mph, a 400 lb maximum user weight, a 1.25 HP continuous and 3.5 HP peak motor, powered incline, a 69.2 lb unit weight, a two-year warranty and 30-day returns. The capacity and belt figures matched the Amazon listing and independent coverage when we checked them in October. MERACH gives 12% incline while a published roundup describes 10%, so the card leaves the maximum out.",
  },
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 2, name: "Brands", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/brands/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 3, name: "RHYTHM FUN Treadmill", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/rhythm-fun-treadmill/" },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is RHYTHM FUN a good treadmill brand?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "It is a budget brand with its own US store and real prices. Its 13% incline walking pad with handlebars has speed, capacity and belt figures that an independent listing summary confirms; the rest of the current range rests on RHYTHM FUN’s figures alone, and the warranty is one year.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the best RHYTHM FUN walking pad?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "On 5 November 2026, the RHYTHM FUN 13% Incline Walking Pad with Handle Bar at $209.99: 0.6 to 6.2 mph, a 300 lb capacity, a 37.5 by 15-inch belt, a 3.9-inch folded height and three manual incline positions to 13%, with side handlebars.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Are the RHYTHM FUN AP01 and AP02 discontinued?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "They appear to be. A walking-pad tracker recorded both as removed from Amazon US by February 2026, and neither was in RHYTHM FUN’s store catalogue on 5 November 2026. Any you find for sale are likely old stock or second-hand.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Does the RHYTHM FUN incline treadmill have a motorised incline?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "No. Every RHYTHM FUN treadmill and walking pad on its store on 5 November 2026 used a manual incline, set by hand at two or three fixed positions with the belt stopped, from 8% on the S8 to 13% on the current walking pads.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Has there been a RHYTHM FUN treadmill recall?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "We searched the CPSC recall database for RHYTHM FUN by title and manufacturer on 5 November 2026 and found no recall. Check your model number on the CPSC site, since marketplace products are sometimes recorded under another company’s name.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is RHYTHM FUN the same as YPOO?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "They appear to be linked. RHYTHM FUN’s pads use the YPOOFIT app, and its S8 treadmill’s description calls it a “YPOO treadmill”. We could not confirm that individual models are identical, but it is worth comparing a RHYTHM FUN with any YPOO pad that shares its photos and figures.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      author: { "@type": "Person", "@id": "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/editorial-standards/#shaheer-ali-khan", name: "Shaheer Ali Khan", honorificSuffix: "PhD", jobTitle: "Exercise Physiology & Biomechanics Specialist", url: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/editorial-standards/#who", sameAs: ["https://www.linkedin.com/in/shaheer-ali-khan-data/"], worksFor: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Treadmill Reviews USA" } },
      datePublished: "2026-11-05",
      dateModified: "2026-11-05",
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Treadmill Reviews USA" },
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      ...(product.brand ? { brand: { "@type": "Brand", name: product.brand } } : {}),
      ...(product.image ? { image: product.image.url } : {}),
      ...(product.priceBounds
        ? {
            offers: {
              "@type": "AggregateOffer",
              priceCurrency: "USD",
              lowPrice: product.priceBounds.low,
              highPrice: product.priceBounds.high,
              url: product.amazonUrl,
            },
          }
        : {}),
    })),
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: 5,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: `https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/rhythm-fun-treadmill/#${product.id}`,
      })),
    },
  ],
};

// "More in" links: the other pages in this article's hub group.
const HERE = "/rhythm-fun-treadmill/";
const HUB = SECTIONS.find((s) => s.slug === "brands");
const GROUP = HUB?.groups.find((g) => g.pages.some((p) => p.url === HERE)) ?? HUB?.groups[0];
const MORE = (GROUP?.pages ?? []).filter((p) => p.url !== HERE).slice(0, 6);
---

<Layout
  title={`${title} | Treadmill Reviews USA`}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: "2026-11-05",
    modifiedTime: "2026-11-05",
    author: "Shaheer Ali Khan",
    section: "Fitness Equipment",
    tags: [
      "rhythm fun treadmill",
      "rhythm fun walking pad treadmill",
      "rhythm fun incline foldable treadmill",
      "rhythm fun walking pad",
      "rhythm fun treadmill review",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{ text: "Closest on Amazon: RHYTHM FUN 6.2MPH Walking Pad with Handles, 16% Incline", link: PRODUCTS[0].amazonUrl }}
>
  <article class="ed-article" data-template="editorial">
    <div class="ed-col">
      <nav class="ed-crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <a href="/brands/">Brands</a>
        <span aria-hidden="true">›</span>
        <span>Brand Guide</span>
      </nav>
      <h1 class="ed-title">RHYTHM FUN Treadmill: What We Could Confirm</h1>
      <p class="ed-dek">
        RHYTHM FUN sells incline walking pads and small folding treadmills on Amazon and through its own small US store. The pads most people remember, the AP01 and AP02, have gone; the current range is built around a 13% manual incline. We read RHYTHM FUN’s store on 5 November 2026 and checked what we could against independent trackers. Here is what holds up and what does not.
      </p>

      <!-- Byline -->
      <div class="ed-byline">
        <img src="/authors/shaheer-ali-khan.png" alt="Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD" width="44" height="44" />
        <div class="ed-byline-text">
          By <a href="/editorial-standards/#who" rel="author">Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD</a><br />
          Updated November 5, 2026 · Exercise Physiology & Biomechanics Specialist · ACSM-EP, CSCS
        </div>
        <a href="#top-picks" class="btn-ghost">View products</a>
      </div>
      <p class="ed-disclosure">
        We research every product on this page independently. If you buy through our links, we may earn a
        commission at no cost to you. <a href="/editorial-standards/">Learn more about how we review.</a>
      </p>

      <figure class="ed-hero">
        <div class="ed-hero-collage">
          <a href="#rhythm-fun-16-incline-walking-pad-handles"><img src="https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/41W9q1HnBxL._SL500_.jpg" alt="RHYTHM FUN 6.2MPH Walking Pad with Handles, 16% Incline" width="478" height="500" decoding="async" /></a>
          <a href="#deerrun-a6-plus"><img src="https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/51lOM7bW9jL._SL500_.jpg" alt="DeerRun A6 Plus" width="500" height="500" decoding="async" /></a>
          <a href="#ancheer-walking-pad-handle-bar"><img src="https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/41CghfnkBcL._SL500_.jpg" alt="ANCHEER Walking Pad with Handle Bar and 12% Incline" width="500" height="500" decoding="async" /></a>
        </div>
        <figcaption>RHYTHM FUN 6.2MPH Walking Pad with Handles, 16% Incline · DeerRun A6 Plus · ANCHEER Walking Pad with Handle Bar and 12% Incline</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <div class="ed-body prose prose-lg">
        <p class="ed-lead">
          RHYTHM FUN is a budget walking-pad brand that made its name on Amazon with a pair of small
          incline pads, the AP01 and AP02, that reviewers liked for being quiet and compact. Those have
          been withdrawn, and the brand now sells a 13% incline walking pad with side handlebars, a
          folding 3.0 HP treadmill with handles, a cheaper handle-bar pad and an 8% treadmill with a
          Bluetooth speaker. Its own store lists prices and figures, which is more than many marketplace
          brands offer, but very little of it has been checked by anyone else. This guide sets out what
          we could confirm, where the figures conflict, and how the range compares with checked
          alternatives.
        </p>

        <section id="top-picks" class="ed-toppicks not-prose">
          <h2>Our top picks:</h2>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <span class="tp-label"><span class="tp-award">Closest on Amazon:</span> <a href="#rhythm-fun-16-incline-walking-pad-handles">RHYTHM FUN 6.2MPH Walking Pad with Handles, 16% Incline</a></span>
              <a href={PRODUCTS[0].amazonUrl} target="_blank" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener" class="buy-pill">Amazon &rarr;</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="tp-label"><span class="tp-award">Closest on Amazon:</span> <a href="#deerrun-a6-plus">DeerRun A6 Plus</a></span>
              <a href={PRODUCTS[1].amazonUrl} target="_blank" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener" class="buy-pill">Amazon &rarr;</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="tp-label"><span class="tp-award">Closest on Amazon:</span> <a href="#ancheer-walking-pad-handle-bar">ANCHEER Walking Pad with Handle Bar and 12% Incline</a></span>
              <a href={PRODUCTS[2].amazonUrl} target="_blank" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener" class="buy-pill">Amazon &rarr;</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="tp-label"><span class="tp-award">Most Features:</span> <a href="#rhythm-fun-s8">RHYTHM FUN S8 Treadmill with 8% Incline</a></span>
              <a href={PRODUCTS[3].amazonUrl} target="_blank" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener" class="buy-pill">Amazon &rarr;</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="tp-label"><span class="tp-award">Best Checked Alternative:</span> <a href="#merach-novawalk-w50-trekpad">MERACH NovaWalk W50 TrekPad</a></span>
              <a href={PRODUCTS[4].amazonUrl} target="_blank" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener" class="buy-pill">Amazon &rarr;</a>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <aside class="ed-note not-prose">
          <span class="ed-note-k">What we checked</span>
          <strong class="text-gray-900">What we checked, and what we have not done.</strong> On
          5 November 2026 we read the full product catalogue of RHYTHM FUN’s US store
          (rhythmfunfitness.com: five treadmills and walking pads and one exercise bike), with each
          model’s price, stock status and description. We cross-checked the models against OCDevel’s
          walking-pad tracker, which recorded RHYTHM FUN’s earlier AP01, AP02 and CP03 in February 2026,
          and against an independent listing summary of the current 13% handle-bar pad that reads its
          Amazon listing. Amazon and the international reseller pages that copy its listings returned
          errors or blocks, so we could not confirm Amazon prices or sellers. We found no hands-on review
          of any current RHYTHM FUN model. Figures that only RHYTHM FUN gives are marked as the maker’s,
          and figures that conflict are kept off the cards. On 9 November 2026 we added RHYTHM FUN’s
          Amazon listing for its side-handlebar pad, read from Amazon’s product data, and replaced two
          RHYTHM FUN models we could not find on Amazon US with the DeerRun A6 Plus and ANCHEER’s
          handle-bar pad, whose figures are those checked for our 2-in-1 and ANCHEER guides. We searched the CPSC recall database for
          RHYTHM FUN by title and manufacturer and found no recall. We have not tested any of these
          machines. Prices change often. We earn a commission if you buy through the Amazon links on
          this page; it does not change what we say.
        </aside>

        <nav class="ed-toc not-prose" aria-label="In this guide">
          <h2>In this guide</h2>
          <ol>
            <li><a href="#short-answer">Is a RHYTHM FUN Treadmill Any Good? The Short Answer</a></li>
            <li><a href="#who-is-rhythm-fun">Who Makes RHYTHM FUN Treadmills?</a></li>
            <li><a href="#models">RHYTHM FUN Treadmills and Walking Pads: The Current Range</a></li>
            <li><a href="#incline-foldable">The RHYTHM FUN Incline Foldable Treadmill, Explained</a></li>
            <li><a href="#discontinued">What Happened to the RHYTHM FUN AP01 and AP02?</a></li>
            <li><a href="#what-to-check">What to Check Before Buying a RHYTHM FUN</a></li>
            <li><a href="#who-should-buy">Who Should Buy a RHYTHM FUN Treadmill</a></li>
            <li><a href="#faq">Frequently asked questions</a></li>
          </ol>
        </nav>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Is a RHYTHM FUN Treadmill Any Good? The Short Answer</h2>
<p>
            <strong>RHYTHM FUN’s 13% incline walking pad with side handlebars is a reasonable budget pad
            for incline desk walking, and its main figures hold up against an independent source; the rest
            of the range rests on RHYTHM FUN’s word alone.</strong> Buy at the store price or from a
            listing Amazon fulfils, keep to walking and light jogging, and do not expect much from the
            one-year warranty.
          </p>
          <p>
            RHYTHM FUN sits in the middle of the budget walking-pad market. It has its own US web store
            with real prices, which many marketplace brands lack, and the AP01 and AP02 pads it sold until
            early 2026 gathered well over a thousand <a href="/how-to-read-treadmill-reviews/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>Amazon reviews</a>, mostly praising how quiet and compact they
            were. But it publishes no motor ratings for its pads, gives some figures only in the product
            title, lists one treadmill twice at prices $130 apart, and has no independent hands-on reviews
            of its current models that we could find.
          </p>
          <p>
            The cards below cover the RHYTHM FUN treadmills and walking pads on its store on
            5 November 2026, or the closest Amazon listings where Amazon does not sell a model, with the
            maker’s figures labelled as such, and one checked alternative. If you
            are looking for the AP01 or AP02, the <a href="#discontinued" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">section on discontinued models</a> explains what happened to them.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-is-rhythm-fun" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Who Makes RHYTHM FUN Treadmills?</h2>
<p>
            RHYTHM FUN sells from rhythmfunfitness.com and on Amazon. On 5 November 2026 its store listed
            six products: four treadmills and walking pads, a duplicate listing of one of them, and an <a href="/best-exercise-bikes/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>exercise bike</a>. It does not say who builds its machines, and like most budget walking-pad brands
            it almost certainly sources them from a factory that supplies other labels.
          </p>
          <p>
            There is one clue. RHYTHM FUN’s pads use the YPOOFIT app, and the description of its S8
            treadmill calls it a “YPOO treadmill”, the name of another walking-pad brand on Amazon. That
            suggests RHYTHM FUN and YPOO share machines, copy or both, which is common in this market. It
            means a YPOO pad with the same photos, belt and incline may be the same machine under another
            name, sometimes at a different price, and it is worth comparing the two before you buy. Our
            guide to <a href="/buying-a-treadmill-on-amazon/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">buying a treadmill on Amazon</a> explains how to compare listings that look alike.
          </p>
          <p>
            RHYTHM FUN’s pads and treadmills are listed with a one-year warranty, which OCDevel’s tracker
            also recorded for the AP models along with a 30-day return policy. That is shorter than the 18
            to 24 months several rival brands give. Warranty claims on an Amazon purchase go through the
            seller in the buy box.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="models" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>RHYTHM FUN Treadmills and Walking Pads: The Current Range</h2>
<p>
            These are the four RHYTHM FUN machines on its US store on 5 November 2026, at the store’s
            prices, plus one checked alternative. Only the 13% handle-bar pad had its main figures
            confirmed by a second source; on the other cards, “maker’s figures” means exactly that.
            Where sources disagree, the figure is left off the card and the description says so. RHYTHM
            FUN sells these models on its own store; on Amazon US we could match only its side-handlebar
            incline pad, listed there with a 16% rather than 13% incline, so the 3.0 HP treadmill and the
            cheapest handle-bar pad are shown by the closest machines Amazon does sell, and the text
            below still describes the RHYTHM FUN models.
          </p>
          <p>
            For incline desk walking with handlebars, the 13% pad (on Amazon, its 16% listing). For light
            jogging, the 3.0 HP treadmill with handles at its lower listed price on RHYTHM FUN’s store, or
            the DeerRun A6 Plus on Amazon. For the cheapest handle-bar pad, the $199.99 model, or ANCHEER’s
            handle-bar pad on Amazon. The
            S8 is hard to justify at its price. And for a longer, wider belt and checked figures, the MERACH
            W50 TrekPad.
          </p>
        </div>
